DevOps and Cloud(AWS) Interview Questions Explained — One at a Time!

𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗼 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗲/𝘀𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗲/𝗵𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗹𝗲 𝘀𝗲𝗰𝗿𝗲𝘁𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗝𝗲𝗻𝗸𝗶𝗻𝘀 ? 
Ans : Secrets are never stored in plain text, and never hard-coded.
𝟭. 𝗝𝗲𝗻𝗸𝗶𝗻𝘀 𝗵𝗮𝘀 𝗮 𝗯𝘂𝗶𝗹𝘁-𝗶𝗻 𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗿𝘆𝗽𝘁𝗲𝗱 𝗰𝗿𝗲𝗱𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗮𝗹 𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗲
Types of secrets stored:
 • Username + Password
 • API Keys
 • SSH Keys
 • Secret Text
 • Certificates
 • AWS Access Keys

𝟮. 𝗨𝘀𝗮𝗴𝗲 𝗶𝗻 𝗣𝗶𝗽𝗲𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲: 
withCredentials([string(credentialsId: 'my-api-key', variable: 'API_KEY')]) {
  sh 'echo "Using API key: $API_KEY"'
}

𝟯. 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗴𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗘𝘅𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗮𝗹 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝗿𝗲𝘁 𝗠𝗮𝗻𝗮𝗴𝗲𝗿𝘀 :
Common Integrations:
 • HashiCorp Vault
 • AWS Secrets Manager
 • Azure Key Vault
 • Google Secret Manager
𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗛𝗮𝘀𝗵𝗶𝗖𝗼𝗿𝗽 𝗩𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁:
withVault([vaultSecrets: [[path: 'secret/app', secretValues: [
 [envVar: 'DB_PASS', vaultKey: 'password']
]]]]) {
  sh 'echo $DB_PASS'
}

𝟰. 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝗿𝗲𝘁𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗗𝗲𝗰𝗹𝗮𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗣𝗶𝗽𝗲𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘀: Use environment with credentials binding:

pipeline {
 environment {
  AWS_CREDS = credentials('aws-cred-id')
 }
 stages {
  stage("Deploy") {
   steps {
    sh "aws s3 ls --access-key \$AWS_CREDS_USR --secret-key \$AWS_CREDS_PSW"
   }
  }
 }
}
